  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 PM CDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nBroad 500-hPa ridging remains across across the majority of the\nCONUS, although the ridge has flattened out just a bit over the\nprevious 24 hours or so. Several shortwaves can be noted moving\nthrough the upper-level flow, with the most notable ripple being\nlocated over eastern NE, northeastern KS, northwestern MO, and\nwestern IA. This shortwave is providing enough synoptic level\nforcing to produce some light showers across northern MO early\nthis afternoon. While not super impactful, the showers, and\nassociated cloud cover, are working to keep temperatures from\nclimbing towards the previously forecasted mid-to-upper 90s. As\nsuch, have capped afternoon high temperatures to the upper 80s\nand low-90s north of the Missouri River. While dew points are in\nthe low-to-mid 70s, heat indices north of the Missouri River are\nonly expected to climb into the upper 90s and low-100s this\nafternoon, so the Heat Advisory has been cancelled. South of the\nshowers and cloud cover, temperatures are still on track to\nreach the mid-to-upper 90s with heat indices around and above \n105F, so the Extreme Heat Warning remains in effect through 9 PM\nthis evening.\n\nAt the surface, a stationary front remain positioned across\nnortheastern KS and northern MO. This will be the focus point\nfor any potential convection this evening and overnight. In a\nsimilar fashion to previous nights, most CAMs depict convection\nblossoming across eastern KS and northern MO as the LLJ\ninteracts with the boundary. Expectation is for storms to\ndevelop between 02Z and 05Z and last through much of the\novernight. Assuming storms are able to form(50-70% chance along\nand north of the Missouri River), they will have 2500-3500 J/kg\nof MUCAPE to work with. However, deep layer shear values are \nonly expected to be on the order of 15-20 kts, so organized \nconvection may be a bit more difficult to come by. This does not\nmean severe weather is impossible as storms may still be able \nto produce downbursts as they pulse up and down, so strong to \ndamaging winds are possible tonight. PWATs also remain on the \norder of 2-2.5\", which will allow storms to be very efficient \nrainfall producers and may lead to locally heavy rainfall \nbeneath the strongest cores. This presents a flash flooding \nthreat, particularly if storms are able to train over the same \nareas.\n\nThe front will begin to slide south overnight tonight, pushing\nthrough the KC Metro early tomorrow morning. Most of the hi-res\nguidance suggests that the front will exit the southern portion\nof the CWA by mid-to-late morning tomorrow bringing much needed\nrelief from the heat and humidity. However, with the front\nprogressing through the area, there will likely be a gradient \nin high temperatures, with low-90s possible across the southern \nportion of the CWA to low-80s further north. A few elevated \nshowers and storms are possible (25-35% chance) across the area \nSunday afternoon, but no severe weather is anticipated.\n\nMonday will see the coolest temperatures in quite some time with\nhighs topping out in the mid-80s. Precipitation chances are also\nnear zero on Monday for the first time in a while with the cold\nfront having fully exited the area. However, this brief benign\npattern will be short lived as another shortwave moves across\nthe Northern and Central Plains on Tuesday with an associated \nsurface front dropping south towards the area. Differences in \ntiming, placement, and intensity of the shortwave still exist \nbetween model suites (both deterministic and ensemble). However,\na general consensus seems to be present regarding the \nenvironment ahead of the system, with 2000-3000 J/kg of MUCAPE \nand 25-35 kts of deep layer shear ahead of the surface front. \nThis parameter space points to a potential for organized \nconvection and the chance for severe weather. This thinking is \nreflected in the SPC Day 4 outlook, which has the northern third\nof the area in a Slight Risk (Level 2 of 5) for severe weather.\n\nModel guidance diverges through the remainder of next week,\nhowever, it seems as though most point towards a northwest flow\npattern setting up across the northern and central Plains, which\nmay lead to a more active stretch of weather.",
